title: Mount points
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: overview
aliases: mount mounts volume unmounted
summary: A removable volume inside a repository is recorded as a mount point; while it is unplugged its subtree is frozen — unavailable, never deleted.

A directory of a watched tree can be the mount point of a removable or network volume —
a USB key, an external drive, an SSHFS export. Unplugged, the directory is still there,
just empty: nothing in its content tells that apart from "everything inside was
deleted". So metafolder records mount points explicitly, and must never conclude from an
empty one that a thousand tracked files are gone.

A tracked path has three states, not two:

* ''present'' — the file is there;
* ''absent'' — the file is gone: `mfr_path = nothing` (only the watcher, `mf orphan clear` and forced writes record that);
* ''unavailable'' — the volume holding it is not mounted. Nothing is recorded: the metarecord keeps its path, its metadata is frozen, and the subtree is skipped by everything until the volume is back ([[Offline subtrees]]).

The third state is never stored on the metarecords themselves: it is derived, when asked,
from the mount point above them — a directory carrying `mfr_mount` ([[mfr_mount]]).
Mount points are detected, not declared: mount the volume and reconcile, and it is
recorded.

